WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:06.080
Well, it's here at last, and it seems the
first episode of The Mandalorian has dived

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.250
deeper into the Star Wars galaxy than anyone
could have expected.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.160
Although the pilot for the first live-action
Star Wars series is paced slowly enough, things

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.290
really kick into gear during the back quarter
of the show's very tidy 39 minutes, and the

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:23.910
episode's conclusion served up a truly exciting
treat of a final twist.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.800
The best place to start here is probably with
the elephant in the room, or, more accurately,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.730
the little green baby.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:34.640
Anybody who's engaged with pop culture in
the last four decades or so will immediately

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.510
recognize the little tyke in the floating
pram as being of the same species as Yoda,

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:44.290
the inimitable Jedi Master who schooled Luke
Skywalker in the ways of the Force.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:49.840
Now, The Mandalorian is set five years after
the fall of the Empire, so Yoda is of course

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.710
dead, having passed away during the events
of Return of the Jedi.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.850
Here's the thing, though: through the many
decades of expanded universe novels, comics,

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:02.930
and encyclopedias based on Star Wars, Yoda's
species has never actually been given an official

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.140
designation, at the insistence of George Lucas
himself.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:11.330
You may recall that The Phantom Menace briefly
introduced Yaddle, another member of Yoda's

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:14.990
species, but even then the species wasn't
given a name.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.350
Lucas likely did this in the past to maintain
the mystery of Yoda's character, and as far

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:24.700
as the narrative of The Mandalorian is concerned,
this could turn out to have been a wise choice.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.370
Since Star Wars is transitioning away from
the Skywalkers and their story, it feels like

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:34.200
the right time to pull the curtain away from
the mystery of Yoda's people, both as an interesting

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.030
expansion of the galaxy's existing lore, and
as a means of injecting The Mandalorian with

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:40.930
some well-timed hype.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:45.950
The secondary surprise behind this literal
last-minute twist, is, of course, the fact

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:51.530
that this crucial target for bounty hunters
across the galaxy is a baby kept under heavy

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.470
guard on a backwater boulder of a planet.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:58.530
Also noteworthy is the difference between
the instructions given to the Mandalorian

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.159
and his fellow bounty hunter, the droid IG-11.

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:06.880
While the Mandalorian is heavily-incentivized
to bring his bounty in alive, IG-11's instructions

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.129
were explicitly to kill, no questions asked.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:11.980
"This is true."

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.279
They are members of the same guild, but were
obviously contracted by two different parties

00:02:17.279 --> 00:02:20.689
with very different interests surrounding
the Yoda-like baby.

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:25.719
So, if even the remnants of the evil Empire
aren't particularly keen to assassinate a

00:02:25.719 --> 00:02:30.459
baby, who could possibly want the little green
guy straight-up dead?

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:35.769
Perhaps more importantly, who could possibly
be so invested in the life of this baby that

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:39.769
they'd place it on a distant planet, guarded
by dozens of men?

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:43.819
Of course, it's totally possible that the
heavy security guarding the baby was put up

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:48.559
by the New Republic, although this isn't made
crystal clear, and it could be down to some

00:02:48.559 --> 00:02:51.280
totally different benefactor, too.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.829
It's also possible that there are different
factions of the former Empire vying against

00:02:55.829 --> 00:03:00.999
each other with the specific interests of
either protecting or killing the child.

00:03:00.999 --> 00:03:05.250
After the end of the Battle of Endor, the
Empire became fragmented into a number of

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.299
smaller groups competing for power and influence.

00:03:08.299 --> 00:03:13.099
It's also entirely possible that another one
of these groups will come into play in future

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:17.030
episodes, with our bounty hunters and their
young quarry caught in the middle.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.340
Then you've got the Mandalorian himself.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:24.079
The show's protagonist was given a surprising
amount of backstory right up front in this

00:03:24.079 --> 00:03:27.169
episode, but his motivations still remain
very murky.

00:03:27.169 --> 00:03:32.590
For example, he refuses to take Imperial credits
as payment for a mission, even though it's

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:38.620
stated they still have monetary value, but
he has no problem taking a face-to-face bounty

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.269
from an Imperial.

00:03:40.269 --> 00:03:46.069
He's also clearly depicted as a hyper-competent
and ruthless warrior who always gets his man,

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:48.139
no matter what.

00:03:48.139 --> 00:03:50.279
"Look, there must be some mistake.

00:03:50.279 --> 00:03:51.680
I can get you more credits."

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:57.799
"I can bring you in warm, or I can bring you
in cold."

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:01.559
But at the end of the episode, he's shown
tentatively reaching out with a finger to

00:04:01.559 --> 00:04:03.829
catch the baby's attention.

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:09.709
This character, all at once a hitman, an orphan,
and a fellow just trying to get by, has only

00:04:09.709 --> 00:04:14.650
just been introduced, and already it's clear
that there's much more to him beyond his capacity

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.380
for hunting and killing.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.320
It seems certain that the mystery of what
lies underneath that helmet will be a major

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.160
focus of the first season of The Mandalorian.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.760
With the first episode in the bag, though,
only one thing is certain: if you were a helpless

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.340
baby alien stranded alone with half the galaxy
out to kill you, this is definitely the guy

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.540
you'd want as your protector.

